# Cloudflare Pages header rules — same intent as the Caddyfile prepared
# for the (currently unused) Railway config, adapted to Cloudflare's own
# _headers syntax, which is explicitly modelled on Netlify's format.
#
# Specific rules are listed before the broad catch-all. Both Cloudflare's
# and Netlify's own docs describe matching by most-specific pattern, and
# this ordering is ALSO correct if it instead resolves by first-match
# top-to-bottom — the one model it would be wrong under is a same-header
# "last rule wins" merge, which isn't how either platform documents this
# feature. Worth a direct check after the first deploy regardless (curl
# -I a font URL and a page URL, confirm each gets the Cache-Control below
# and not the other's) — Cloudflare Pages isn't available in this
# environment, so this file's real-world behaviour is unverified here.
#
# Fonts and OG images only ever change by getting a new filename in
# practice (a new case gets a new OG image; an existing one is only ever
# replaced deliberately) — safe to cache for a year. Everything else
# (every page, whether the URL literally ends in .html or is a clean
# directory-style path like /work/some-slug/, plus every .css and .js)
# changes on a deploy without its filename changing, so it stays
# no-cache: the browser still keeps a local copy and revalidates it
# cheaply (a 304 when nothing changed) rather than re-downloading, but
# never risks serving yesterday's page after a deploy fixes something.

/*.woff2
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/assets/og/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache
